## Problem

The nushell wrapper's `--execute` directive call wasn't wrapped in `try`. Nushell 0.98+ raises a `ShellError` on a non-zero external exit, so a `--execute` body that exits non-zero unwound the whole `def` — cleanup never ran, all three `mktemp` files leaked, and the stdout the function still had to return was discarded. Cleanup has always sat after the exec call, so this is pre-existing.

Verified end-to-end against nushell 0.114.1 (the version CI pins) driving the real rendered wrapper in a dedicated `TMPDIR`:

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| `wt switch --create … --execute "echo BODY; exit 42"` | exit 42, **3 temp files leaked** | exit 42, 0 leaked |
| `wt switch --definitely-not-a-flag` (wt itself fails) | exit 2, 0 leaked | unchanged |
| happy path (`--execute "echo OK"`, then `wt config show`) | exit 0, stdout returned, 0 leaked | unchanged |

The bare mechanism, with no wrapper involved:

```nu
def wrapper [] {
    let f = (mktemp --tmpdir)
    if true { ^sh -c "exit 42" }
    print "REACHED_AFTER_EXEC"     # never prints
    "RETVAL"                       # never returned
}
```

### Why the existing test stayed green

`test_wrapper_execute_exit_code_propagation` already drives this exact command for `nu` and passes — but it passes because the unwind *happens* to carry exit 42 out to the shell, not because the wrapper handled it. The assertion the test makes is satisfied by the failure mode, so the leak sat on a path that looked covered.

## Solution

Wrap the exec call in `try`, the same way the binary call above it already is, and fold the body's exit code into `$exit_code`:

```nu
let exec_exit = if ($exec_file | path exists) and (open $exec_file --raw | str trim | is-not-empty) {
    let script = open $exec_file --raw
    try { ^sh -c $script; 0 } catch { $env.LAST_EXIT_CODE }
} else {
    0
}

let exit_code = if $exit_code != 0 { $exit_code } else { $exec_exit }
```

The end of the function then propagates it through the existing `^sh -c $"exit ($exit_code)"` path, so the 42 is the wrapper's doing rather than a side effect of the unwind — and cleanup runs on the way.

#3733 flagged the precedence question — when *both* `wt` and the body fail, which code wins — as a user-visible contract rather than a mechanical port. It turns out the contract is already set: [bash](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/286004f1ebe5f6563c772be24987394144a29215/templates/bash.sh#L51-L57), [zsh](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/286004f1ebe5f6563c772be24987394144a29215/templates/zsh.zsh#L54-L60) and [fish](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/286004f1ebe5f6563c772be24987394144a29215/templates/fish.fish#L54-L61) all guard their `src_exit` assignment with `if $exit_code -eq 0`, i.e. first non-zero wins, wt before the body. This mirrors that rather than inventing a nushell-specific rule, which is what took it from an issue to a PR.

`^sh -c $script` keeps its non-terminal position inside the `try` block, so the body's

